    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>’s richest viceroyalty, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city">Mexico City</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philippines">Philippines</a>.</li><li>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, coined as the Spanish dollar and carried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a>, linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>.</li><li>Hidalgo’s revolt of 1810 began a long war, and the Plan of Iguala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-cordoba">Treaty of Córdoba</a> ended Spanish rule in 1821.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a> sailed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acapulco">Acapulco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila">Manila</a> for 250 years, from 1565 to 1815, carrying Mexican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> and returning with Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porcelain">porcelain</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

