    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hernan-cortes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes.jpg" alt="Hernán Cortés" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Hern%C3%A1n_Cort%C3%A9s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artistUnknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> was born from the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> landed on the Gulf coast in 1519, founded Veracruz, and with the decisive help of indigenous allies, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a>, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, which fell on 13 August 1521 after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic had already devastated its defenders. Cortés named the conquered lands New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city">Mexico City</a> built on the ruins of the Aztec capital. Conquistadors spread out to subdue Oaxaca, Michoacán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a> and the Gulf coast, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nuno-de-guzman">Nuño de Guzmán</a>’s brutal campaigns in the 1530s created the Kingdom of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galicia">Galicia</a> in the west. The Crown moved quickly to curb the power of the conquerors. It created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a>, a royal court and governing council, in 1527, and in 1535 appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a> as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, who governed until 1550 and laid the foundations of colonial administration, founding the Mexico City mint and supporting the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#printing-press">printing</a> press in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>, which began work in 1539. Franciscan, Dominican and Augustinian friars arrived from 1524 to convert the indigenous population. Over the following decades the viceroyalty’s authority came to extend over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a>, the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a>, the northern frontier provinces and, after 1565, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philippines">Philippines</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#zacatecas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zacatecas.jpg" alt="Zacatecas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#zacatecas">Zacatecas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campo_en_zona_arqueol%C3%B3gica_La_Quemada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ErickBasM, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The conquest was followed by demographic catastrophe. Epidemics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#smallpox">smallpox</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#measles">measles</a> and the diseases known as <em>cocoliztli</em>, especially in 1545–1548 and 1576–1580, together with war and forced labour, reduced the indigenous population of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a> by perhaps 90 per cent by the early seventeenth century, although the size of the original population is disputed. Indigenous communities nevertheless survived as self-governing towns that paid tribute and supplied labour, first through the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#encomienda">encomienda</a></em> and later the <em>repartimiento</em>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">Silver</a> transformed the economy after the discoveries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> in 1546 and Guanajuato soon after; the patio process of amalgamation with mercury, developed by Bartolomé de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#medina">Medina</a> at Pachuca in the 1550s, made mass production possible. Silver went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a> in the convoys from Veracruz and across the Pacific in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acapulco">Acapulco</a>, where it bought Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porcelain">porcelain</a>. Large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hacienda">haciendas</a> raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>, wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maize">maize</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a> estates relied partly on enslaved Africans, perhaps 200,000 of whom were brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, mostly before 1650. A layered society emerged of Spaniards born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, American-born <em>criollos</em>, indigenous people, Africans and mixed groups classified as <em>castas</em>. The Catholic Church was the central institution of cultural life, and the devotion to the Virgin of Guadalupe, said to have appeared to Juan Diego in 1531, became a shared focus of identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#diego-de-vargas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas.jpg" alt="Diego de Vargas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ddevargas.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of its history <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> kept only a small regular army, relying on conquistadors, militias and indigenous allies. Resistance did not end with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. In the Mixtón War of 1540–1542 the Caxcanes and other peoples of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galicia">Galicia</a> rose against Spanish rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> Mendoza himself led the army that crushed them with thousands of indigenous auxiliaries. The discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> in the north provoked the Chichimeca War, a long conflict along the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city">Mexico City</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> from about 1550 to 1590, which the Spaniards ended less by force than by a policy of gifts, settlement and missions. On the northern frontier, a line of presidios, or garrisoned forts, protected missions and mining towns against Apache and Comanche raids, and the Pueblo Revolt of 1680 drove the Spaniards out of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a> returned in 1692. At sea, the fortress of San Juan de Ulúa guarded Veracruz, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#piracy">pirates</a> led by Laurens de Graaf sacked the port in 1683. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> captured Havana in 1762, the Crown built a regular army and an extensive colonial militia in New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>. These forces, commanded by royalist officers such as Félix María Calleja, fought the insurgents during the war of independence from 1810, defeating Miguel Hidalgo at the Bridge of Calderón in January 1811.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#council-of-the-indies"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies.jpg" alt="Council of the Indies" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Emblem_of_the_Council_of_the_Indies.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown Author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> was the king’s personal representative, combining the roles of governor, captain-general and president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a>. He answered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, and at the end of his term his conduct was examined in a judicial review, the <em>residencia</em>. Other audiencias, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a> from 1543, Guadalajara from 1548 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila">Manila</a> from 1583, exercised judicial and administrative authority within the viceroyalty, and Guatemala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuba">Cuba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philippines">Philippines</a> were ruled by captains-general who were largely autonomous in practice. Provinces were run by <em>corregidores</em> and <em>alcaldes mayores</em>. The law distinguished a republic of Spaniards from a republic of Indians, whose towns kept their own councils and nobles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, Mixtec and other languages were used in local records. The Crown held extensive rights over the Church under the royal patronage, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> established a tribunal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in 1571. Laws issued over two centuries were compiled in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#recopilacion-de-leyes-de-los-reinos-de-las-indias">Recopilación de Leyes de los Reinos de las Indias</a></em> of 1680. In the eighteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a>, driven by the inspector-general José de Gálvez from 1765, reorganised the colony: a royal tobacco monopoly, the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1767, the Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces in 1776 and twelve intendancies in 1786 increased revenue and central control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mexico-city.jpg" alt="Mexico City" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city">Mexico City</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sobrevuelos_CDMX_HJ2A4913_(25514321687)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gobierno CDMX, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> was the most valuable part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and a hinge of the first global economy. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, coined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city">Mexico City</a> mint as the <em>real de a ocho</em> or Spanish dollar, circulated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> and became a standard trade coin across the world, later serving as a model for the currencies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a> and China. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a>, sailing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acapulco">Acapulco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila">Manila</a> from 1565 to 1815, connected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> directly for two and a half centuries. Crops, animals and techniques were exchanged across the Atlantic and Pacific, carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cacao">cacao</a> and chillies outward and introducing wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>, horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a>. Culturally, New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> produced a distinctive baroque civilisation: the cathedrals of Mexico City and Puebla, the poet and scholar Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz, the polymath Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora, and the Royal University of Mexico, founded in 1551. Indigenous and Spanish traditions mixed in religion, art and language, while the Virgin of Guadalupe became a lasting symbol. The viceroyalty shaped the borders, legal systems and cities of modern Mexico and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a>, and left Spanish place names, missions and law across the south-western United States, from Texas to California, as well as a lasting Hispanic and Catholic imprint on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philippines">Philippines</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> reached its peak of wealth around 1800, when it had some six million inhabitants, its mines were producing more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> than ever, and Alexander von Humboldt described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city">Mexico City</a> as one of the finest cities built by Europeans. Yet resentment was growing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> favoured Spanish-born officials over criollos, and the Consolidation of 1804 forced the Church to call in loans, damaging landowners and merchants. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a> forced the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> to abdicate in 1808, Spanish merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a> City overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> José de Iturrigaray to prevent criollo moves towards self-government. On 16 September 1810 the priest Miguel Hidalgo launched a mass revolt at Dolores, which took Guanajuato but was defeated in 1811; Hidalgo was executed. José María Morelos continued the struggle, convening the Congress of Chilpancingo in 1813, until his capture and execution in 1815. Royalist armies then contained the insurgency, which survived as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a>. The end came from within the royalist camp. After liberal revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> in 1820, the royalist officer Agustín de Iturbide agreed with Guerrero on the Plan of Iguala of February 1821, promising independence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#catholicism">Catholicism</a> and equality. On 24 August 1821 the newly arrived Juan O’Donojú signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-cordoba">Treaty of Córdoba</a>, and in September Iturbide’s army entered Mexico City, founding the First Mexican Empire.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> was the richest and most populous viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philippines">Philippines</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city">Mexico City</a> for three centuries; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> and Pacific <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galleon">galleons</a> helped to create the first global economy, and its society laid the foundations of modern Mexico.</em></p>

